<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    What makes a .vxml compile by being in the project. Imported automatically by a
    PackageReference to Vixen.Ui; inside this repository a project has to name the generator
    itself, because analyzers are not transitive through a ProjectReference.
-->
<Project>

    <!--
        ⚠ **A flag saying this file has been imported, and the repository's `Directory.Build.targets`
        is what reads it.** Inside this tree there are no packages, so that file stands in for the
        `PackageReference` — and it is imported *after* everything a project writes, including an
        explicit `Import` of this file at the bottom of a `.csproj`. A project that already names
        this file therefore sets the flag first, and the automatic wiring stays out of its way rather
        than declaring the two globs below a second time and failing on a duplicate item.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <VixenUiBuildImported>true</VixenUiBuildImported>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        A .targets rather than a .props, and for the same reason the Vixen.Sdk pair records: this is
        imported *after* the consuming project's body, which is when DefaultItemExcludes finally
        holds the project's own additions to it. Globbed in a .props, a .vxml copied into bin/ by a
        previous build would be compiled a second time under a different path — two classes with one
        name, and an error naming neither the file the author wrote nor the reason.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(EnableVixenMarkup)' != 'false'">
        <AdditionalFiles Include="**/*.vxml" Exclude="$(DefaultItemExcludes);$(DefaultExcludesInProjectFolder)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        ⚠ **The other half of the pair, and it did not exist until doc 43's `@theme` work.** A .vxml
        compiled and a .vcss did not: there was no item type, no glob and no loader for one, so the
        only stylesheet on disk in the whole tree — the MMO sample's `hud.vcss` — was embedded by
        hand and read back through GetManifestResourceStream in a file whose own comments call
        itself a placeholder. Everything else was a `const string` inside a .cs, which is how
        `ControlTheme`, `AdvancedTheme` and `EditorTheme` came to be 3 260 lines of CSS edited
        through a raw string literal.

        ⚠ **Every hand-authored sheet in the tree now arrives through this glob** — ten of them,
        across nine projects, which is why five projects that had no .vxml at all gained an Import of
        this file. A project reaching a stylesheet by GetManifestResourceStream and *not* importing
        this .targets compiles perfectly and throws on first use, so each of those loaders names this
        import in its exception message.

        An EmbeddedResource and not an AdditionalFiles, because a stylesheet is *content* rather than
        compiler input: nothing generates code from it, the assembly carries it so a shipped game has
        no build artefact to deploy, and the logical name is the default — RootNamespace plus the
        folder path — which is what lets a caller name it without MSBuild knowing anything.

        ⚠ And it is what finally gives the `hot-reload DIR` switch something to watch. That has always
        globbed `*.vcss` and for most of its life there was not one in a source tree for it to find,
        so the style half of hot reload was reachable and untested. There are ten now — but the
        origin mismatch it has always had is untouched by that, and is still what stops a *deleted*
        rule disappearing. See `Editor/Vixen.Editor.Host/README.md`.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(EnableVixenStyleSheets)' != 'false'">
        <EmbeddedResource Include="**/*.vcss" Exclude="$(DefaultItemExcludes);$(DefaultExcludesInProjectFolder)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        The two the generator asks MSBuild for. RootNamespace and the folders below the project
        directory are what a component's namespace is built from — the same convention a
        hand-written .cs file in the same folder already follows — and the project directory is also
        what keeps a generated file's hint name relative, so two machines building the same source
        produce the same compilation.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="RootNamespace" />
        <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="ProjectDir" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        ==============================================================================================
        ⚠ **VX4002 — the glob ran and nothing is on the other end of it, which is the worse of the two
        ways to be half-wired.**

        A project with *neither* the import nor the generator at least has a `.vxml` that is not an
        item, and `Directory.Build.targets`'s VX4001 is what says so. A project with the import and
        no generator is the shape `Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph` was in: the `Import` had been added for
        the `**/*.vcss` glob above — under a comment saying "there is no `.vxml` in this project, so
        the other half of the .targets is inert here" — so the day a `.vxml` appeared the glob made
        it `AdditionalFiles`, no analyzer read it, and the build failed with "does not contain a
        definition for" on every member the markup declares. Identical symptom, and the file the
        compiler blames is the hand-written half of a partial class that is correct.

        **The check is one item and one comparison, and both halves of it are load-bearing.** The
        `.vxml` has to be *compiler input* — an `AdditionalFiles` item, which is what the glob above
        produces — and `Vixen.Ui.Markup.Generators.dll` has to be in `@(Analyzer)`, which is the one
        place the two worlds meet. Inside this repository the generator arrives as a
        `ProjectReference` with `OutputItemType="Analyzer"`; outside it, it travels in the package's
        `analyzers/dotnet/cs/`. Both are `@(Analyzer)` items by the time `ResolveReferences` has
        finished, so a check that reads that item is a check that is true in both worlds — verified
        by asking a real build for `-getItem:Analyzer` rather than by reading the SDK.

        ⚠ **After `ResolveReferences` and before `CoreCompile`, and neither end of that is optional.**
        `@(Analyzer)` is empty until `ResolveReferences` has run — a check earlier than that reports
        every project as broken — and after `CoreCompile` the thirty C# errors have already been
        printed and this one is the last line of a wall nobody reads to the bottom of.

        An error rather than a warning, for the reason the whole diagnostic exists: the build is
        going to fail anyway, and it is going to fail naming the wrong file. A warning ahead of that
        wall buys a line in a log. Worse, a `.vxml` with *no* hand-written partner compiles cleanly
        and produces no component at all — silence, discovered at run time — so there is no reading
        of this state in which the build is fine.
        ==============================================================================================
    -->
    <Target Name="VixenCheckMarkupCompilerIsLoaded"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences"
            Condition="'$(VixenUiMarkupCheck)' != 'false'">
        <ItemGroup>
            <_VixenMarkupInput Include="@(AdditionalFiles)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.vxml'" />
            <_VixenMarkupCompiler Include="@(Analyzer)"
                                  Condition="'%(Filename)%(Extension)' == 'Vixen.Ui.Markup.Generators.dll'" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <!--
            Reported against the `.csproj` and not against the markup, which is the entire point:
            every other message this state produces names a `.vxml` or the `.cs` beside it, and the
            mistake is in neither.
        -->
        <Error Condition="'@(_VixenMarkupInput)' != '' AND '@(_VixenMarkupCompiler)' == ''"
               Code="VX4002"
               File="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)"
               Text="Vixen: this project hands @(_VixenMarkupInput->Count()) .vxml file(s) to the compiler — @(_VixenMarkupInput->'%(Identity)', ', ') — and the VXML compiler is not loaded, so nothing reads them and the classes they declare will not exist. Vixen.Ui.targets is imported here, which is what made them AdditionalFiles, but nothing in this compilation's analyzer list is Vixen.Ui.Markup.Generators.dll. Inside the Vixen repository the one line that fixes it is &lt;VixenUi&gt;true&lt;/VixenUi&gt; in this project's first PropertyGroup, which brings the item types and both generators; outside it, a PackageReference to Vixen.Ui carries the generator in analyzers/dotnet/cs and nothing needs saying. If markup here is deliberately not compiled, set &lt;VixenUiMarkupCheck&gt;false&lt;/VixenUiMarkupCheck&gt; and write down why." />
    </Target>

    <!--
        ==============================================================================================
        ⚠ **VX4003 — the *other* generator, which is the half nothing was watching.**

        There are two, and the check above only ever asked about one of them. `Vixen.Ui.Generators`
        turns a `[UiProperty]` into a property the style system can see and carries
        `ComponentParameterAnalyzer` (VXS0320), which is what tells a component author that a plain
        property is not a parameter. Both travel in the package's `analyzers/dotnet/cs/` beside the
        markup compiler, so **outside this repository this state cannot happen** — a
        `PackageReference` to `Vixen.Ui` brings both or neither.

        ⚠ **Inside it, seven projects reached master naming the markup generator and stopping there**,
        and the reason is legible in the diff that created each of them: the comment beside the
        reference says "analyzers are not transitive through a ProjectReference" and then names *the
        VXML compiler*, because a `.vxml` was the thing being wired that day. The sentence is right
        and the item list under it was short by one. Nothing failed, because the consequence of the
        missing generator is silence in both directions: a `[UiProperty]` declared there emits no
        registration and is simply invisible to the cascade, and VXS0320 reports nothing rather than
        reporting nothing wrong.

        Conditioned on the same `.vxml` input as VX4002 rather than on the mere presence of this
        import, deliberately: a project that imports this file for the `**/*.vcss` glob alone has no
        markup and no components, and failing it would be failing it for a generator it has no use
        for. That leaves one hole this check does not cover — a project with `[UiProperty]` and no
        markup at all — and that one is invisible from MSBuild, because whether a `[UiProperty]`
        exists is a question about C# source.
        ==============================================================================================
    -->
    <Target Name="VixenCheckPropertyGeneratorIsLoaded"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences"
            Condition="'$(VixenUiMarkupCheck)' != 'false'">
        <ItemGroup>
            <_VixenComponentInput Include="@(AdditionalFiles)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.vxml'" />
            <_VixenPropertyGenerator Include="@(Analyzer)"
                                     Condition="'%(Filename)%(Extension)' == 'Vixen.Ui.Generators.dll'" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <Error Condition="'@(_VixenComponentInput)' != '' AND '@(_VixenPropertyGenerator)' == ''"
               Code="VX4003"
               File="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)"
               Text="Vixen: this project compiles @(_VixenComponentInput->Count()) .vxml file(s) and Vixen.Ui.Generators is not loaded, so any [UiProperty] declared here generates nothing and is invisible to the style system, and VXS0320 — the analyzer that catches a component parameter written as a plain property — never runs. There are two generators and this project names only the VXML compiler. Inside the Vixen repository the one line that fixes it is &lt;VixenUi&gt;true&lt;/VixenUi&gt; in this project's first PropertyGroup, which brings both; a project that wires itself by hand adds a second ProjectReference to Core/Vixen.Ui.Generators/Vixen.Ui.Generators.csproj with OutputItemType=&quot;Analyzer&quot; beside the one it already has. Outside it, a PackageReference to Vixen.Ui carries both in analyzers/dotnet/cs and nothing needs saying. If markup here is deliberately not compiled, set &lt;VixenUiMarkupCheck&gt;false&lt;/VixenUiMarkupCheck&gt; and write down why." />
    </Target>

</Project>
